WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A short reference episode you can jump straight to later: agent, workflow, or chat — how to pick, in one screen.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.052
One job, one role, done repeatedly — that's an agent.

3
00:00:09.052 --> 00:00:17.000
You define its scope once, and it handles every instance of that job the same way, whether it's triggered by you, a schedule, or an event.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.261
A fixed sequence where each step's output feeds the next — that's a workflow.

5
00:00:22.261 --> 00:00:28.000
You know the shape in advance: gather, then group, then write, always in that order.

6
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.855
Open-ended, needs judgment about what to do next based on what it finds — that's a chat with tools.

7
00:00:33.855 --> 00:00:39.000
Nothing about the path is fixed in advance; the model decides its next move as it goes.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.169
Side by side: agent for a repeated role, workflow for a known fixed sequence, chat for open-ended judgment.

9
00:00:43.169 --> 00:00:51.000
When you're not sure which one fits, ask whether you could write the steps down in advance — if yes, workflow; if the steps depend on what's discovered, chat; if it's the same job over and over, agent.

10
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:01.000
If none of that fits — if you genuinely don't know what step two will be until step one finishes — that's the sign you want a chat with tools, not a workflow forced into a shape it doesn't have.

11
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.333
With the decision made, the next question is running things unattended — safely.

12
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:07.000
That's schedules, starting with the guardrails you set up first.

